<div class="atlas-label"><span>FIELD NOTES / 001</span><span>HIGH POINT, NORTH CAROLINA · 2018</span></div>
<h1>Mapping<br/><em>Educational Access.</em></h1>
<p class="atlas-lead">What happens when the geography of school transportation and the geography of where families live no longer align?</p>
<div class="atlas-credit"><span>ORIGINAL RESEARCH / PUBLIC POLICY / SPATIAL ANALYSIS</span><span>Co-authored by Makenzie Brockel, Avery Moon & Andrew DeHaan</span></div>
</div></header>
<div class="atlas-ribbon"><div class="container atlas-ribbon-grid"><div><b>2018</b><span>Research year</span></div><div><b>18.91% → 21.71%</b><span>Fairview mobility, 2013–2017, as reported in original paper</span></div><div><b>3</b><span>Policy intervention areas</span></div></div></div>
<section class="atlas-section"><div class="container atlas-split"><div><span class="atlas-index">01 / THE QUESTION</span><h2>A move across town shouldn't automatically mean a change of school.</h2></div><div class="atlas-prose"><p>In a collaborative undergraduate research project at High Point University, our team studied student transiency—frequent movement between schools—in Guilford County, North Carolina. We focused on Fairview Elementary School, where the historical mobility rate cited in our paper rose from 18.91% in 2013 to 21.71% in 2017.</p><p>Students' educational continuity is shaped by more than school boundaries. Housing instability, family resources, street safety, access to transportation, and the clarity of reassignment policies can influence whether a student can remain at the same school after moving.</p><p class="atlas-note">Historical research context: figures and recommendations on this page reflect the 2018 paper and have not been updated to describe current district conditions.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="atlas-section atlas-dark"><div class="container"><div class="atlas-map-heading"><div><span class="atlas-index">02 / THE GEOGRAPHY</span><h2>One city.<br/>Overlapping barriers.</h2></div><p>A conceptual spatial diagram of the relationships examined in the research—not a georeferenced reconstruction of 2018 routes or attendance boundaries.</p></div>
<div class="atlas-map" role="img" aria-label="Conceptual map-style visualization showing transportation access as an intersection of housing moves, school attendance zones, walking areas and bus networks. Not geographic data.">
<div class="atlas-map-grid"></div><div class="atlas-orbit atlas-o1"></div><div class="atlas-orbit atlas-o2"></div><div class="atlas-orbit atlas-o3"></div><div class="atlas-route atlas-r1"></div><div class="atlas-route atlas-r2"></div>
<div class="atlas-pin atlas-p1"><i></i><span>HOME & HOUSING</span></div><div class="atlas-pin atlas-p2"><i></i><span>FAIRVIEW ELEMENTARY</span></div><div class="atlas-pin atlas-p3"><i></i><span>TRANSPORT ACCESS</span></div>
