</div><p class="atlas-map-foot">The original materials include historical bus-route images and research notes. Those can be incorporated as properly labeled archival figures after provenance and reuse are confirmed.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="atlas-section"><div class="container"><span class="atlas-index">03 / RESEARCH APPROACH</span><h2>From individual stories to systemic constraints.</h2><div class="atlas-method"><div><small>01</small><h3>Historical data</h3><p>Compared reported school mobility trends and attendance-related context, with Fairview as a focal case.</p></div><div><small>02</small><h3>Stakeholder input</h3><p>Documented discussions with school personnel and the county's transportation leadership.</p></div><div><small>03</small><h3>Spatial reasoning</h3><p>Examined bus-route options, walking/non-transport areas, school assignment and residential mobility together.</p></div><div><small>04</small><h3>Policy review</h3><p>Analyzed how information about school reassignment could be easier for families to find and understand.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="atlas-section atlas-warm"><div class="container"><span class="atlas-index">04 / THE PROPOSALS</span><h2>Three opportunities to improve continuity.</h2><div class="atlas-proposals"><div><b>01 — TRANSPORTATION</b><h3>Reconsider how routes connect students to schools.</h3><p>The team explored rerouting, multi-school transportation and hub-and-shuttle concepts to help children remain at their existing schools after a move. These were proposals, not confirmed implemented services.</p></div><div><b>02 — NON-TRANSPORT AREAS</b><h3>Reassess geographic eligibility and practical access.</h3><p>The research questioned whether existing non-transportation zones adequately reflected safety, distance and students' needs, and considered extending transportation within those areas.</p></div><div><b>03 — POLICY ACCESS</b><h3>Make reassignment information findable.</h3><p>The paper recommended clearer naming and distribution of school-reassignment information so families could better understand options for educational continuity.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="atlas-section"><div class="container atlas-split"><div><span class="atlas-index">05 / WHAT THIS WORK DEMONSTRATES</span><h2>Research is useful when someone can act on it.</h2></div><div class="atlas-prose"><p>This project combined quantitative context, qualitative inquiry, transportation constraints, and public-policy research into a set of concrete recommendations. It also illustrates an enduring interest in how complex administrative systems affect the choices available to communities.</p><p>The original study was collaborative. The paper identifies Makenzie Brockel, Avery Moon, and Andrew DeHaan as co-authors; this portfolio presentation is a contemporary reinterpretation of that shared work.</p><p><strong>Outcome distinction:</strong> the documents reviewed establish the team's analysis and proposals. They do not independently verify full implementation, a subsequent change in mobility rates, or causation. Any documented policy response can be added once a suitable primary record is checked.</p></div></div></section>
